{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 film directed by david fincher that tells the story of the founding of facebook, the social media giant that has become an integral part of modern life. the movie is based on the book \"the accidental billionaires\" by ben mezrich, which chronicles the rise of mark zuckerberg, the co-founder and ceo of facebook, from his harvard days to the company's early success.\n\nthe film begins with mark zuckerberg (played by jesse eisenberg), a sophomore at harvard, being dumped by his girlfriend, erica albright (played by rooney mara). in a fit of anger and hurt, mark creates a website called \"facemash\" that allows users to compare the photos of two harvard students and vote on which one is more attractive. the site becomes an instant hit, but also gets mark into trouble with the university administration.\n\nlater, mark is approached by twin brothers cameron and tyler winklevoss (played by armie hammer), who are members of the harvard rowing team and part of the university's elite social circle. they ask mark to create a social networking site for harvard students, exclusive to those with .edu email addresses. mark agrees, and with the help of his friend eduardo saverin (played by andrew garfield), creates \"thefacebook\".\n\nas the site gains popularity, mark and eduardo become embroiled in a dispute over the direction of the company, with eduardo wanting to keep the site focused on its harvard roots and mark wanting to expand it to other colleges and universities. meanwhile, the winklevoss twins claim that mark stole their idea and sue him for intellectual property theft.\n\nthroughout the film, fincher's direction is masterful, using a combination of quick cuts, clever camera angles, and a pulsating soundtrack to bring the story to life. the cast is also excellent, with eisenberg delivering a nuanced and complex performance as the troubled and ambitious mark zuckerberg.\n\none of the strengths of the film is its exploration of the complexities of mark's character. on the surface, mark is a brilliant programmer and entrepreneur, but as the story unfolds, we see a more nuanced and flawed individual. he is driven by a desire for success and recognition, but also struggles with social awkwardness and a lack of empathy for those around him.\n\nthe film also raises important questions about the ethics of social media and the consequences of creating a platform that can be used to manipulate and exploit others. as mark's creation becomes increasingly popular, we see the darker side of its impact, from the way it can be used to humiliate and embarrass others to the way it can be used to manipulate people's perceptions of themselves.\n\noverall, the social network is a thought-provoking and engaging film that offers a fascinating glimpse into the early days of facebook and the complex and often fraught personalities of its founders. with its talented cast, masterful direction, and timely themes, it is a movie that is sure to continue to resonate with audiences for years to come.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -1.2056629657745361}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.00038092033355496824}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.011048096232116222}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.924481987953186}, {"token": "201", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.00036244976217858493}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.0005529781919904053}, {"token": " by", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " david", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.10195189714431763}, {"token": " tells", "logprob": -0.0011999557027593255}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " story", "logprob": -1.072883